   "source": "Anchor: the filer's own filed depreciation (us-gaap Depreciation, financials_history.csv da column) \u2014 FY2023 $33,000,000, FY2024 $39,300,000, FY2025 $35,400,000; the value used is the top of that filed FY2023\u2013FY2025 range, FY2024's $39,300,000, as the maintenance-capex proxy. No capex value was ever filed: EDGAR companyfacts for CIK 74260 contains no PaymentsToAcquirePropertyPlantAndEquipment (or any candidate capex tag) fact in any period, and ORI's Consolidated Statements of Cash Flows present no purchases-of-property-and-equipment line \u2014 investing activities are maturities/sales/purchases of securities, other investments, and short-term investments, with premises/equipment spend folded into 'Other investments' / 'Other \u2014 net' and not isolable from the filing.",
   "reason": "DATA_GAP:capex \u2014 capex missing for FY2023, FY2024, FY2025 (and every other year) under every candidate tag. The depreciation-anchor exception (Andrew, 2026-10-18) applies: for capex only, when no capex value was ever filed, a capex estimate may anchor on the filer's filed depreciation, within 0.1x\u201310x of that anchor; $39,300,000 is 1.0x the FY2024 filed depreciation anchor (and within 0.1x\u201310x of every filed FY2023\u2013FY2025 depreciation figure). Maintenance capex for an insurer (premises, IT hardware/software, leasehold) is proxied by its own depreciation at the top of the filed range, the conservative end. The figure is immaterial to the verdict: $39.3M is about $0.16/share against the $3.28/share payable-earnings base actually scored, and that base is built from net operating income, which already carries the depreciation charge; the override's binding effect is to unblock the engine's OCF history.",
